     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: **Do Not Accept As-Is \u2014 Negotiate**\n\nYou are in an ideal negotiating position:\n- **You were passively recruited:** You were not looking and are happy at Meta.\n- **Level parity / lateral move:** IC6 at Meta maps to L6 (Senior PM) at Google.\n- **Comp is virtually lateral on an annualized basis:** On paper, an annualized comparison is **$552k/yr** (Google) vs. **$526k/yr** (Meta). A ~5% bump is not enough to leave an established track record and team at Meta.\n- **There is no sign-on bonus** and the base salary is slightly lower than what you already make ($235k vs. $240k).\n\nYou should **not walk away**, but you should **definitely counter**. Google expects candidates at L6 from Meta to negotiate, and their first offer leaves substantial room on the table.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Breaking Down the Numbers\n\n#### A. Annualized Comparison\n| Component | Meta (Current) | Google Offer | Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000** |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000** |\n| **Annual Equity (Avg)** | ~$250,000 | $270,000 ($1.08M / 4) | **+$20,000** |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | $0 |\n| **Total Annualized** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **+$26,000 (+5%)** |\n\n#### B. The Reality of Google\u2019s Vesting Schedule (GSU Front-Loading)\nGoogle\u2019s standard new-hire equity vests **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** over 4 years:\n- **Year 1:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k GSU = **~$638,400**\n- **Year 2:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $356.4k GSU = **~$638,400** *(+ any Y1 refresher)*\n- **Year 3:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $237.6k GSU = **~$519,600** *(+ Y1/Y2 refreshers)*\n- **Year 4:** $235k base + $47k bonus + $129.6k GSU = **~$411,600** *(+ Y1/Y2/Y3 refreshers)*\n\n*Note on equity:* The front-loaded structure gives you strong cash flow in Years 1 and 2, but it creates a steep drop in Years 3 and 4 that relies heavily on annual refreshers to bridge. \n\n---\n\n### 3. How the Offer Ranks in Google\u2019s L6 PM Bands (SF Bay Area)\n\n- **Base ($235k):** Mid-band. The posted band is $183k\u2013$271k. At L6, top-of-band base salaries typically reach **$245k\u2013$255k**.\n- **Bonus (20%):** Standard for L6 (fixed).\n- **Equity ($1.08M / 4 yrs):** Around the **60th percentile** for L6 PM. Top-of-band/strong-hire initial grants for L6 PMs frequently land between **$1.2M and $1.35M**.\n- **Sign-on Bonus ($0):** Weak. At L6, Google routinely offers **$50,000 to $100,000+** in sign-on cash to unseat talent from Meta/Apple.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\nWhen countering, ask for improvements across three levers with the goal of hitting **~$620k+ annualized / ~$700k+ Year 1**:\n\n1. **Sign-on Bonus:** Ask for **$100,000** (Target: settle at $60k\u2013$80k).\n   * *Rationale:* You are walking away from an upcoming $200k Meta vest in the next 12 months, plus an established performance cycle.\n2. **Base Salary:** Ask for **$255,000** (Target: settle at $245k\u2013$250k).\n   * *Rationale:* You cannot take a step down in guaranteed base pay ($240k at Meta vs. $235k at Google).\n3. **Equity (GSUs):** Ask for **$1,280,000 over 4 years** ($320k/yr avg) (Target: settle at $1.15M\u2013$1.2M).\n   * *Rationale:* Closes the gap and ensures Years 3 & 4 remain competitive even before refreshers stack.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Strategy & Script\n\nBecause you are happy at Meta, you hold all the leverage. Frame the counter as: *\"I'm excited about the team, but as a passive candidate, the current numbers don't provide enough financial incentive to justify walking away from my current trajectory at Meta.\"*\n\n#### Email / Phone Script to Recruiter:\n\n> *\"Thank you for extending the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and the product space is genuinely exciting to me.*\n>\n> *I took time to evaluate the full compensation package against my current situation at Meta. Because I wasn't actively looking and have a strong trajectory here, compensation is a major factor in my decision. Right now, on an annualized basis ($552k vs. my current $526k), the offer is essentially lateral, and the base salary is actually lower than my current $240k base.*\n>\n> *Additionally, I'd be leaving behind $200k in unvested equity vesting over the next 12 months, along with my established refresher pipeline.*\n>\n> *To make this an easy decision for me to sign, here is where we would need to land:*\n> - **Base Salary:** *$255,000*\n> - **Sign-on Bonus:** *$100,000*\n> - **Equity:** *$1,280,000 GSU grant over 4 years*\n>\n> *If we can get the package to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to sign and give my notice at Meta right away.\"*\n\n### Summary\nGoogle will not pull an offer for a polite, well-reasoned counter from an L6 candidate. If they meet you halfway (e.g., $245k base, $1.2M equity, $75k sign-on), Year 1 compensation will exceed **$700,000**, making the move financially worthwhile. If they refuse to budge on all three levers, you can comfortably stay at Meta.",
