    "message": {
     "content": "**Yes, you should strongly consider taking this offer.** \n\nFrom a purely financial standpoint, Google met your exact equity target ($1.28M) and matched your Meta base salary ($240k). While they declined the cash sign-on bonus, **the way Google structures equity makes this a top-tier L6 PM offer that heavily outpays your current Meta comp over the next 2\u20133 years.**\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Real Numbers (Front-Loaded Vesting)\n\nGoogle vests new-hire equity over a **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** schedule. Because of this front-loading, your Year 1 and Year 2 compensation will be dramatically higher than Meta:\n\n| Year | Base | Target Bonus (20%) | GSU Vesting | Total Annual Comp (Google) | Total Annual Comp (Meta) | Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1 (33%)** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $422,400 | **$710,400** | ~$476,000\u2013$526,000 | **+$184,000 to +$234,000** |\n| **Year 2 (33%)** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $422,400 | **$710,400** *(+ Y1 refresh)* | ~$526,000 | **+$184,000+** |\n| **Year 3 (22%)** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $281,600 | **$569,600** *(+ Y1/Y2 refresh)* | ~$526,000 | **+$43,000+** |\n| **Year 4 (12%)** | $240,000 | $48,000 | $153,600 | **$441,600** *(+ Y1-Y3 refresh)* | ~$526,000 | *(Depends on refresh)* |\n\n#### Why the missing sign-on bonus doesn't break the deal:\nGoogle's standard first offer was $270k/yr flat equivalent. By bumping you to $1.28M with a 33% front-load, **you are taking home $422,400 in stock alone in Year 1.** \n\nThat is **$152,400 more equity in Year 1** than their initial offer. In practice, Google effectively rolled the sign-on bonus directly into your first two years of equity.\n\nOver the next **24 months**, you will earn roughly **$1.42M+ at Google** vs. **~$1.05M at Meta**\u2014an extra **~$370,000** in your pocket.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What Happens in Years 3 and 4?\n\nAt Google, L6 PM annual refreshers typically range from **$110,000 to $180,000/yr** (depending on performance ratings), each vesting over multi-year periods.\n- By **Year 3**, stacked refreshers generally add **$70,000\u2013$110,000/yr**, keeping your total comp near **$640k\u2013$680k**.\n- By **Year 4**, stacked refreshers will add **$130,000\u2013$180,000/yr**, keeping your total comp comfortably above **$570k\u2013$620k**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Evaluating the Move Beyond Just Comp\n\nYou now have a near top-of-band L6 compensation package. Your final decision comes down to team culture and career goals:\n\n| Factor | Meta (IC6) | Google (L6) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Pace & Agency** | Move fast, high autonomy, metrics-obsessed, direct ownership. | Slower, consensus-driven, heavy stakeholder alignment. |\n| **Performance Pressure** | High PSC pressure; stack ranking requires constant high output. | More sustainable work-life balance; less volatile cycles. |\n| **Resume & Network** | Proves you can execute fast and deliver aggressive business KPIs. | Adds blue-chip Big Tech variety; opens up VP/Director tracks outside FAANG later. |\n\n---\n\n### Final Recommendation\n\n* **Accept the offer** if you are open to a change of pace, want to diversify your resume with both Meta and Google at the Senior PM level, and want to lock in a guaranteed **$710k+ / year for the next two years**.\n* **Decline only if** you are on an active promotion track to IC7 (Director/Principal PM) at Meta within the next 12\u201318 months, or if you strongly dislike Google's consensus-heavy culture.",
